Delta Pack (ADR-0016) against real S3, with a same-machine local-store control.

host:     AWS c6a.8xlarge, 32 vCPU, us-east-1 (no KVM needed: this is a pure
          blockd benchmark, so it runs on a cheap instance rather than metal)
store:    temporary in-region S3 bucket; control is a file store on the root EBS
build:    release, this branch
date_utc: 2026-07-27

blockd/tests/commit_cost.rs, median of 3, serial. "sparse" is one dirty 64 KiB
page per chunk -- the shape a 4 KiB random-write Guest produces, and what delta
packs change. "dense" is every page dirty, which still publishes full 4 MiB bases
and is therefore both the control and a stand-in for the old behaviour on ANY
dirty chunk.

--- real S3 -------------------------------------------------------------------

dense (full 4 MiB bases):
dirty chunks  published MiB     flush ms   ms per pub MiB
           8          32.00        858.9            26.84
          32         128.00       3031.5            23.68
          48         192.00       4913.5            25.59

sparse (delta packs):
dirty chunks  dirty pages  published MiB     flush ms   ms per pub MiB   sha256 MiB/s
           1            1           0.06        125.4          2006.73           1227
           8            8           0.50        374.0           748.09           1695
          16           16           1.00        630.3           630.33           1566
          32           32           2.00       1149.4           574.69           1656
          48           48           3.00       1669.3           556.43           1685

--- control: same machine, local file object store -----------------------------

dense:
           8          32.00        166.4             5.20
          32         128.00       1974.0            15.42
          48         192.00       2974.7            15.49

sparse:
           1            1           0.06         19.9           318.29           1681
           8            8           0.50         43.8            87.53           1696
          16           16           1.00         76.8            76.78           1654
          32           32           2.00        140.2            70.08           1680
          48           48           3.00        312.6           104.20           1673

--- what this says -------------------------------------------------------------

FLUSH latency, dense -> sparse, same run:

  dirty chunks     on S3            on the local store
             8     858.9 -> 374.0   =  2.3x     166.4 ->  43.8  =  3.8x
            32    3031.5 -> 1149.4  =  2.6x    1974.0 -> 140.2  = 14.1x
            48    4913.5 -> 1669.3  =  2.9x    2974.7 -> 312.6  =  9.5x

**The win is SMALLER on S3, not larger.** This corrects the guess recorded before
this run ("both changes should look better on S3"). Delta packs cut published
bytes 64x but leave the object *count* unchanged -- one object per dirty chunk
either way -- and on S3 an object is a PUT round trip, so the fixed per-object
cost that delta packs do not touch is a much larger share of the total. It shows
up directly in the sparse rows: 556 ms per published MiB at 48 chunks, against
25.6 ms per published MiB for the dense case, i.e. the sparse commit is paying for
round trips, not for bytes.

Golden roots do not have this property (they remove the objects entirely, so they
are 162x on S3 against 102x locally); delta packs do, and it is the clearest
argument yet for the follow-up ADR-0016 already names: pack several chunks'
deltas into one object, so a commit's object count falls with its byte count.

The 64x reduction in published bytes is arithmetic and holds everywhere. What
varies by backend is how much of the flush that buys back.
